            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Consider a prime $p$ of the form $4 t+3$. Show that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ iff $-a$ has order $(p-1) / 2$.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"number_theory\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nSuppose first that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\). Then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Hence \\\\(a^m\\\\) has order \\\\(2\\\\), so \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)\\\\mid m\\\\). Conversely, if \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\na^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(a\\\\) has order \\\\(2m\\\\), this implies \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), hence \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Consequently \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\). Put \\\\(x=-a\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(x\\\\) has odd order \\\\(m\\\\), while \\\\(-1\\\\) has order \\\\(2\\\\). These two orders are coprime. Because \\\\(a=-x\\\\), the commuting elements \\\\(-1\\\\) and \\\\(x\\\\) have product of order\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{lcm}(2,m)=2m=p-1.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1\\\\), so \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
